<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ashraf Umar II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1296</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ashraf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz">Umar II</a> was a sultan of the Rasulid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> who reigned briefly from 1295 to 1296, after his father al-Muzaffar Yusuf I. A noted scholar, he wrote works on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>, including a treatise on the astrolabe, as well as on medicine, agriculture, veterinary science and genealogy, and an astrolabe he made survives in the Metropolitan Museum of Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p></div>
